One approach from @jcheng5:

```R
waitForEventLoop <- function(code, timeout) {
  # recorded by js handler that counts number of times busy event has been fired
  runs <- element$private$event_count()

  code

  # wait until busy cycles increased by one; timing out in case nothing
  # happened
  timeout_by <- proc.time()[[3]] + timeout
  while(proc.time()[[3]] < timeout_by) {
    if (element$private$event_count() > runs) {
      return()
    }
    Sys.sleep(0.1)
  }
  stop("Timed out")
}
```

This has the advantage of not needing the name of an output you expect to be updated.

Still suffers from the downside that if the reactive code runs (e.g.) `insertUI()`, another independent run of the event loop will trigger, and this isn't guaranteed to wait for it.

e.g..

```R
  click = function(id) {
    js <- glue::glue('$("#{id}").click()');
    self$execute_js(js)
  },

  drop_down = function(id, pos = NULL) {
    js <- glue::glue('
      $("#{id}")
        .siblings()
        .filter(".selectize-control")
        .find(".selectize-input")
        .click();
    ')
    self$execute_js(js)

    if (!is.null(pos)) {
      js <- glue::glue('
        $($("#{id}")
          .siblings()
          .filter(".selectize-control")
          .find(".selectize-dropdown-content")
          .children()
          .get({pos - 1}))
          .mouseenter();
        ')
      self$execute_js(js)
    }

    invisible(self)
  },
```

I have been using `shinytest` to automate pointing and clicking things in the app and `testthat` to test that the actual result matches the expected result. I do this because our data changes every day and a snapshot approach does not work. 

Having said that, I just read the _Dealing with dynamic data_ section of the documentation which suggests I take a snapshot of data and use that data when testing like so:

```r
if (isTRUE(getOption("shiny.testmode"))) {
  # Load static/dummy data here
} else {
  # Load normal dynamic data here
}
```
 
My questions are: 
* Where should I put this chunk? 
* Should I use `.rda`/`.rdata` files (asking because of the word "load" in the comments) or can I continue to use `.rds`?

For example, this waits until `input$state` is a value other than NULL or `""`.

```R
for (i in 1:100) {
  state <- try(app$getValue("state", "input"))
  if (inherits(state, "try-error") || identical(state, "")) {
    Sys.sleep(0.2)
  } else {
    break
  }
}
```

As seen in https://github.com/rstudio/shinycoreci-apps/blob/73997616e9d2f6584cded524ad46faf44154d4e2/021-selectize-plot/tests/shinytests/mytest.R

This should also be documented on the website, possibly in the FAQ.

Also, we might need to check how long `getValue` will block for before it times out. Ideally, we'd be able to call it with a short timeout.

Thanks for making out life easier testing shiny apps.
I have a fairly complex shiny app that uses multiple layers of dynamic UI. I found that using `Sys.sleep` makes testing too slow, since sometimes I don't know precisely how long the UI will take to render.

Similar on `app$waitFor` function, I wrote a rudimentary function that checks when an input widget becomes available:

```r
waitforWidget <- function(app, inputname, interval=1, timeout=60) {
  waited <- 0
  while (!inputname %in% app$listWidgets()$input) {
    Sys.sleep(interval)
    waited <- waited + interval
    if (waited > timeout) {
      stop('App failed to load', inputname, ' in ', timeout, ' seconds')
    }
  }
  message('loaded ', inputname)
}
```

Cheers.

